<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Push notifications (FCM/APNs). Must match the provisioning profile's
	     environment. Every CI build is DISTRIBUTION-signed — the ad-hoc
	     `hubmarket-shop-adhoc` profile (build-on-push) and the TestFlight/App Store
	     profiles all carry `aps-environment=production` (get-task-allow=false).
	     A `development` value on a distribution-signed build makes
	     `registerForRemoteNotifications` fail, so APNs hands over no device token
	     (the FCM token then stays null). Use `production`. (Only a local Xcode
	     debug run with a *development* profile would need `development`.) -->
	<!-- APPLE PAY — intentionally NOT declared yet.
	     The wallet code is in place (PaymentChannel.swift, ApplePaySession.swift)
	     but the merchant entitlement is deliberately absent until the external
	     setup is done, because declaring it here without regenerating the
	     provisioning profiles produces a build that fails to install locally and
	     an Apple Pay sheet that cannot be presented on device.

	     To enable, IN THIS ORDER:
	       1. Apple Developer portal -> Identifiers -> Merchant IDs: create
	          merchant.com.hubmarket.app (confirm the exact string with Network
	          International first — it must match what they register).
	       2. Get the Apple Pay payment-processing CSR from N-Genius, upload it
	          against the merchant id, return the certificate to them. Without
	          this the sheet completes but authorisation always fails, because
	          N-Genius cannot decrypt the PKPaymentToken.
	       3. App ID com.hubmarket.app -> enable "Apple Pay Payment Processing" and
	          associate the merchant id.
	       4. REGENERATE BOTH ios/signing/*.mobileprovision (an existing profile
	          never picks up a new capability). Keep the same filenames; keep the
	          ad-hoc profile's device UDIDs; keep the App Store profile free of a
	          ProvisionedDevices list — tool/ios_*_build.sh picks the profile
	          solely on that key.
	       5. Add the key here, and set APPLE_PAY_MERCHANT_ID in config/*.json to
	          the same string.

	     Note CI archives UNSIGNED and re-signs with the PROFILE's entitlements
	     (tool/ios_*_build.sh step 5d), so this file governs local Xcode builds
	     only — the profile is what actually ships. Those scripts assert the two
	     stay in sync once the key is added here.

	<key>com.apple.developer.in-app-payments</key>
	<array>
		<string>merchant.com.hubmarket.app</string>
	</array>
	-->

	<!-- UNIVERSAL LINKS — intentionally NOT declared yet, same reasoning as
	     Apple Pay above: an existing provisioning profile never picks up a new
	     capability, so declaring this key before the portal work makes local
	     Xcode builds fail to sign, and a CI build (which re-signs from the
	     PROFILE) would ship silently WITHOUT it — universal links would just
	     keep opening Safari with no visible error.
	
	     Android already behaves correctly: assetlinks.json is live and the
	     intent filter is scoped, so a hub-market.magento2.click link opens the app when
	     installed and the browser when not. This key is the iOS equivalent.
	     The in-app routing is already done and platform-agnostic
	     (DeepLinkResolverScreen); nothing else in Dart needs to change, and
	     FlutterDeepLinkingEnabled is already true in Info.plist.
	
	     To enable, IN THIS ORDER:
	       1. Publish the AASA file at BOTH
	            https://hub-market.magento2.click/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
	            https://www.hub-market.magento2.click/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
	          Content from docs/backend/apple-app-site-association. It must be
	          served as application/json, with NO redirect and NO file
	          extension. As of 2026-08-22 that path 301s to /uae-en/ (Magento's
	          store-code redirect swallows it) and Apple does NOT follow
	          redirects — so this step is a real server change, not a copy.
	          Verify with tool/verify_applinks.sh.
	       2. Apple Developer portal -> Identifiers -> App ID com.hubmarket.app:
	          enable the "Associated Domains" capability.
	       3. REGENERATE BOTH ios/signing/*.mobileprovision. Keep the same
	          filenames; keep the ad-hoc profile's device UDIDs; keep the App
	          Store profile free of a ProvisionedDevices list — tool/ios_*_build.sh
	          picks the profile solely on that key.
	       4. Uncomment the key below. tool/ios_appstore_build.sh then asserts the
	          signed app actually carries it and fails the build if the profile
	          was not regenerated.
	
	     Team ID 544Y9RU66L; App ID 544Y9RU66L.com.hubmarket.app (from the
	     committed profiles). The AASA appIDs entry must match that exactly.
	
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:hub-market.magento2.click</string>
		<string>applinks:www.hub-market.magento2.click</string>
	</array>
	-->

	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>production</string>
</dict>
</plist>
